# Act One

## Scene 1

[[In a play, # starts an act and ## starts a scene, and both print as headings. Other Fountain apps treat these lines as outline markers and leave them out of the printed script.]]

A kitchen, late at night. A table, two chairs, a telephone on the wall. The clock reads twenty to midnight.

MARGARET, in her sixties, sits at the table in front of a plate she hasn't touched. She watches the telephone.

[[Paragraphs between speeches are stage directions. A play has no INT. or EXT. headings: the set is described in a stage direction instead.]]

MARGARET
(quietly)
You said you'd call.

Silence. She scrapes the plate into the trash. A knock at the back door.

DANNY (offstage)
Mom? It's me. I lost my phone.

[[An offstage voice is marked (offstage), rather than the screenplay's (O.S.).]]

MARGARET
Of course you did.

She opens the door. DANNY, in his thirties and soaked through, holds out a bakery box gone soft in the rain.

DANNY
I *did* call. From a pay phone. Do you know how hard it is to find a pay phone?

MARGARET
(taking the box from him and setting it
on the table without looking inside)
Harder than finding your mother's house, apparently.

[[A direction can wrap onto a second line inside a speech. Drafter keeps it as one parenthetical; other Fountain apps show the second line as dialogue.]]

DANNY
Look inside.

She lifts the lid.

MARGARET
Is that a cake?

DANNY
It was a cake.

They look at each other. She almost laughs.

MARGARET
Sit down before you drip on everything.

He sits. She hands him a dish towel and, after a long moment, a fork. The lights fade.

[[A play has no CUT TO: or other camera directions. A lighting change is written as a stage direction.]]

## Scene 2

The back step, later. The rain has stopped. MARGARET and DANNY eat the ruined cake straight from the box.

DANNY
Next year I'll call from the bakery.

MARGARET
Next year you'll come at seven.

Blackout.

> END OF SAMPLE <

